<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Harness.RigControl</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>rig-control</AssemblyName>

    <!--
      Sharp7 now comes from NuGet, pinned centrally (2026-09-20). The warning this comment used to
      carry — "two projects resolving the same DLL differently is a way to build one half against a
      version the other has never seen" — is exactly what central package management makes
      structurally impossible, which is a large part of why that option won.
    -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THIS BUILD CAN PUT A CPU INTO RUN, AND THE PROJECT FILE IS THE FIRST PLACE THAT IS VISIBLE.

    It is the opposite claim to Harness.RigWrite's, and it is stated here for the same reason: the
    reference list is what a reader checks first.

      - DeviceGuard comes in through Harness.S7, and is the fence. There is no path to a device in
        this assembly that does not pass RunTransitionFence first (RigControlCli, section 1).
      - Harness.S7 is referenced for S7Status and S7RunStateReading — the run-state DECODER
        specifically, which carries a measured fact about this CPU and a regression guard. Reusing it
        is strictly better than a second copy that would drift.
      - Sharp7 is referenced DIRECTLY, because IS7Client deliberately does not expose PlcHotStart and
        must not start doing so. See IRunTransitionTransport for why the verb lives on its own
        interface in its own assembly rather than widening that one.

    What this assembly must NOT be able to reach, checked by an IL walk rather than asserted here:
    PlcStop, PlcColdStart, DBWrite, MBWrite, WriteArea, Download, Delete, PlcCopyRamToRom.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.S7\Harness.S7.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Sharp7" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    SHARP7 IS NOW DEFINED UNCONDITIONALLY, and that is a real change rather than tidying. It used to
    be set only when the machine-local DLL existed, so a build without it excluded
    Sharp7RunTransition.cs and the composition root handed the CLI a factory that threw with a named
    reason. The fence, the CLI and every exit code still built and tested — deliberately, because
    the safety-relevant half of this tool must not depend on being able to reach a device.

    That property still holds and is still worth protecting; what has gone is the BUILD variation.
    The live transport is now always compiled in, so "can this build reach a device" stops being a
    question about which machine ran msbuild. It never should have been one: a #if that changes
    which code exists is how two people run the same command and get tools that differ in what they
    can do to a CPU.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);SHARP7</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
